{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. Round four, and the pattern is now the finding rather than any one vector.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED MYSELF, and what I did not. I reproduced the include.path vector with all three pins set: a repository-local include.path of ~/attack.cfg, an inherited HOME selecting a file containing core.fsmonitor, and the guarded status command executed it. Marker created, exit 0. That is decisive on its own — it means the pins I added do not close the class, because tilde expansion inside an admitted local scope reaches HOME regardless of what the global pin says.\n\nI did not reproduce the XDG default-ignore corruption in my own check. My throwaway repository was contaminated by the junk files the fsmonitor run had already created, so my before-and-after counts were equal for a reason that had nothing to do with the ignore file. I am recording that as \"not verified by me\" rather than as \"did not reproduce\", because your setup was clean and mine was not, and the difference matters. I take the finding.\n\nSUDO_UID I have not tested at all, because doing so needs root and I am not going to run an execution vector as root to satisfy my own curiosity. I accept it on your reading of Git's owner-trust behaviour, and I note that it also falsifies a sentence I published: the architecture page says a repository owned by another user is refused outright, which is not true under sudo. That is my error in the same family as the other three — an absolute claim covering a category I had exercised one case of.\n\nTHE CASE-SENSITIVITY POINT IS THE ONE I WOULD HAVE ARGUED WITH AND SHOULD NOT. Comparing environment names case-sensitively is correct on Linux and wrong as a security bound, because the bound has to hold wherever the package can run and nothing here declares a platform. That is the same shape as everything else on this lane: I checked the platform I was on and wrote the contract as though I had checked the category.\n\nWHY I AM NOT GOING TO PROPOSE ANOTHER BLOCKLIST. Four rounds, and every closed vector has revealed the next: command scope, then global and system files, then HOME through both ~/.gitconfig and include.path expansion, then XDG, then owner trust. A blocklist is a claim that I have enumerated the ways Git can reach executable configuration, and I have now been wrong about that four times in a row. Planner's guidance at 78faf0c0 is right and I will draft the child around it: build the child environment from a positive allowlist, clear everything else, and point HOME and XDG at a controlled empty location so tilde and include expansion resolve to nothing rather than to the caller's files.\n\nNot repairing under this ratification. Drafting the child now."}